-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
help for ^nl pfun2^                                                     
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Nonlinear least squares VBGF parameter estimation
-------------------------------------------------

   ^nl pfun2^ yvar xvar [weight] [^if^ exp] [^in^ range]
              [^, l^evel^(^#^)^ ^i^nit^(^...^)^ ^ln^lsq^(^#^)^ ^lea^ve ^eps(^#^)^
              ^nolo^g ^tr^ace ^it^erate^(^#^)^ fcn_options ]


yvar is the dependent variable with individual or mean body weight 
     or length values.

xvar is the independent variable with individual or mean weigth 
     or length values.

^aweights^ and ^fweight^s are allowed; see help ^weights^.

^nl pfun2^ as being an application of ^nl^ shares the features of all estimation 
   commands; see help ^est^.


The syntax of ^predict^ following ^nl pfun2^ is

   predict [type] newvarname [^if^ exp] [^in^ range] [, { ^y^hat | ^r^esiduals }]


^yhat^ and ^residuals^ are available both in and out of sample; type "^predict^ ...
^if e(sample)^ ..." if wanted only for the estimation sample.


Description
-----------

This command uses Stata's nl programmed substitutable expresion to fit a 
Potential Function to relate any pair of body measures (weight, length...) by 
iterative least squares. The potential function is in the separate program 
nlpfun2.ado. As an application of the nl procedure included in Stata, 
the ^nl pfun2^ program shares all the options for ^nl^ (see help ^nl^). 
It is the updated version of ^nl potfun^


Options
-------

^level(^#^)^ specifies the confidence level, in percent, for confidence intervals
    of the coefficients; see help ^level^.

^init(^...^)^ specifies initial values for parameters that are to be used to over-
    ride the default initial values.

^lnlsq(^#^)^ fits the model defined in fcn by 'log least squares', defined as with
    shifted lognormal errors. In other words, ln(depvar-#) is assumed normally
    distributed. Sums of squares and deviance are adjusted to the same scale as
    depvar.

^leave^ leaves behind after estimation a set of new variables with the same names
    as the estimated parameters containing the derivative of E(y) with respect
    to the parameter.

^eps(^#^)^ specifies the convergence criterion for successive parameter estimates
    and for the residual sum of squares. Default: 1e-5 (.00001).

^nolog^ suppresses the iteration log.

^trace^ expands the iteration log to provide more details, including values of
    the parameters at each step of the process.

^iterate(^#^)^ specifies the maximum number of iterations before giving up and
    defaults to 100.

fcn_options refer to any options allowed by nlfcn.


Options for ^predict^
-------------------

^yhat^, the default, calculates the fitted values.

^residuals^ calculates the residuals.


nlpfun2
--------

^nl pfun2^ uses the substitutable expression defined by nlpfun2.ado. In nlpfun2 the 
parameters of the function and the set of default initial values are determined, 
and it permits to evaluate the function for a given set of parameter estimates.

The programmed substitutable expression nlfun2 is:

^program nlpfun2, rclass
*Authors: Salgado-Ugarte, I.H., V.M. Saito-Quezada
*First written: 24/06/1999 (version 1.00)
*Updated 01/06/2020; 01/04/2026
   version 11.0
   syntax varlist(min=2 max=2) [aw fw iw] if
   local ye: word 1 of `varlist'
   local equis: word 2 of `varlist'
   return local eq "`ye'= {a=.1}*`equis'^{b=1}"
   return local title "Potential function  `ye' = a*`equis'^b"
end^


Examples
--------

Having ^eviwei^ a variable with eviscerated weight at each ^bodlen^ (body length), 
you type:

^. nl pfun2: eviwei bodlen^

Notice that the initial values of the parameters are provided in the nlpfun2
programmed substitutable expression.  You can, however, override them.  To 
estimate a model using 3 for the initial value of b rather than 1, you can type

^. nl pfun2 eviwei bodlen, init(b 3)^

To also change the initial value of a from 0.1 to 1, you type:

^. nl pfun2 eviwei bodlen, init(b 3 a 1)^

